{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["<b>But he was upon the summit of the hill.</b> The verse explains that when Moses was upon the summit of the hill, spreading his hands upward, Aaron was at his right and Hur at his left, and he in the middle, bearing his ten fingers, drawing the force of support through both of them. This parallels the central line through which his prophecy flows, receiving from the seraphim that stand above it. So too we find with the High Priest, that the Segan was at his right and the head of the father's house at his left, and Moses did likewise; but the influx from above comes by way of the line of faith that does battle for them." "<b>And his hands were steady (emunah).</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): it says that when Aaron and Hur supported his hands from either side, his hands were strong; and the word אמונה is from the expression (Isaiah 22:23): ״ותקעתיו יתד במקום נאמן״ — 'and I will fasten him as a nail in a sure place.' For at first, when he would raise his hand, Israel prevailed; and when he let his hand down — meaning, against his will, for he was unable to keep them upright, either because his hands grew heavy or for the reason that our Rabbis, of blessed memory, darshened: 'A person is forbidden to stand with his hands spread toward heaven for three hours' — then Amalek prevailed. Therefore they needed Aaron and Hur to support his hands." "<b>By way of the Midrash</b> — 'And his hands were emunah': Moses leapt to pray in the merit of the patriarchs Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob. אמונה — this is Abraham, as it is said (Genesis 15:6): ״והאמין בה׳״. עד בא — this is Isaac, as it is said (Genesis 24:62): ״ויצחק בא מבוא״. השמש — this is Jacob, as it is said (Genesis 32:32): ״ויזרח לו השמש״ — end of quotation. And the meaning of this midrash, in my opinion, is that since Amalek trusted and relied upon the strength of Esau the wicked, his ancestor, who was blessed with the strength of Isaac — which is the attribute of Gevurah — and wished to overpower Israel by means of this force, therefore Moses needed to outwit him, and he mentioned in his prayer the three patriarchs, for he [Amalek] is a descendant of Jacob, and it [Israel's merit] is the threefold cord in the merit of all three of them. And our Rabbis, of blessed memory, further darshened: ״עד בא השמש״ — all the kingdoms do not wage war except until the sixth hour of the day, but the wicked kingdom wages war morning and evening. And they further darshened of it, that the sun stood still for Moses here: ״עד בא השמש״ — they [the nations] would calculate the hours by astrology. What did Moses do? He set the wheel of the sun and the moon and confounded their hours, as it is said: ״והיה כאשר ירים משה ידו״, and it is written (Habakkuk 3:11): ״רום ידיהו נשא״, and it is written after it: ״שמש ירח עמד זבולה״ — end of quotation, in the Tanchuma. <b>To this man.</b> Now is this not a euphemism of his own [Esau's]? — as one says, 'the betrothed maiden cried out.' And therefore they darshened that so did the prophet state (Obadiah 1:11): ״ביום עמדך מנגד ביום שבות זרים חילו ונכרים באו שעריו ועל ירושלים ידו גורל גם אתה כאחד מהם״ — this teaches you that the brotherhood was already annulled and he departed from its category, and he is like the rest of the foreign nations. For the prohibition of interest was not forbidden to Israel concerning him, only for the reason that we were commanded to sustain him and perform deeds of lovingkindness with him, from what is written (Leviticus 25:36): ״וחי אחיך עמך״." "<b>By way of the Kabbalah</b> — 'And his hands were emunah': Moses would direct his ten fingers and lift his hands toward the height of the heavens, which is the central line called רום with a cholam, and this is the expression ״ירים משה ידו״, and the intent was for Moses, or for the priest in the lifting of the hands. For in spreading the ten upon this intention, all ten would be sanctified, and the influx would be drawn through them and from them to the intelligences, and at that hour the hands of the accusing forces would go slack, and not one of them had the power to accuse; and then Israel would prevail and gain strength, for not one of the forces would flutter a wing or open a mouth or chirp. Therefore ״וגבר ישראל״ with the lifting of his hands, and with the lowering of them ״וגבר עמלק״. And Moses was compelled both in their lifting and in their lowering: in their lifting, so that the accusers would be nullified from their action and Israel would prevail; and in their lowering — for had he not lowered them, the forces would have been nullified entirely and would not perform their appointed action at all, and a person is not permitted to do so, for ״לא תוהו בראה לשבת יצרה״ — 'He did not create it a void, He formed it to be inhabited' — and the forces in their appointment are always needed. And this is the reason for the prohibition of standing for three hours with hands spread toward heaven, and this is clear." "<b>And it says:</b> 'And it was (ויהי)' — and it should have said 'and they were (ויהיו),' but the word ויהי returns to Moses, and its meaning is: 'and Moses was (ויהי משה),' spreading his hands to draw force to the final ה, which is the attribute of judgment that does battle for Israel, which is called אמונה, as in what is written (Psalms 92:3): ״ואמונתך בלילות״. And this is what our Rabbis, of blessed memory, said: 'Anyone who did not say אמת ויציב in the morning and אמת ואמונה in the evening has not fulfilled his obligation' — and the reason is that he did not mention each attribute in its proper time. And it further appears to me that ״ויהי ידיו״ alludes to the practice of the priest who lifts his hands, that he should trace the letters of the Name הוי״ה upon his hands, and this is the expression ״ויהי ידיו״; and his practice further is that his fingers should be upright above with all his might and the intensity of his intention, and that he not join them one to another, and this is the expression אמונה. And the reason is so that each one be blessed above individually, and so as not to mingle below the letters of הוי״ה that he traces upon his fingers. And in the Sefer ha-Bahir you will find: 'His disciples asked him: To whom do we lift hands toward the height of the heavens? From where do we know this? As it is written (Habakkuk 3:10): ״נתן תהום קולו רום ידיהו נשא״ — this teaches that the lifting of the hands is only toward the height of the heavens. And when there are among Israel those who understand, who know the secret of the honored Name, and lift their hands — immediately they are answered, as it is said (Isaiah 58:9): ״אז תקרא וה׳ יענה תשוע ויאמר הנני״. If אז תקרא — HaShem answers immediately, and what is אז? Rather, it teaches that the אל\"ף alone has no permission to be invoked except through the letters of adhesion with it, which sit first in Malkhut, and together with the אל\"ף they are three, leaving seven out of the ten utterances — and these are the seven, and it is written ״אז ישיר משה ובני ישראל״' — also thus, end of quotation from the Sefer ha-Bahir."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 17, :notes {2 "The comment appears to include a separate lemma ('לאיש הזה' / 'To this man') drawn from Deuteronomy 25:17 in connection with the Amalek/Esau midrashic thread; rendered as a bold lemma head following the pattern."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:00:54.469260277Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}